__Using Benthic Macroinvertebrates to Assess the Impacts of Microplastics in Freshwater Ecosystems __

Microplastics (MPs; plastic particles ≤5 mm) are widespread contaminants in freshwater ecosystems, where they can accumulate in sediments and pose a risk to aquatic organisms. Freshwater benthic macroinvertebrates are particularly vulnerable to MPs exposure due to their close association with sediments. Given their importance in cycling nutrients, secondary production and freshwater food webs, understanding the effects of MPs to these organisms is critical to assess risks. Microplastics are a diverse contaminant group that can vary considerably in size, shape, polymer type and condition, and these differences may influence their toxicity, as well as ingestion and retention by freshwater benthic macroinvertebrates. Additionally, MPs exposure may also influence host-associated microbial communities, which are known to play a critical role in overall organism health. My research investigated the effects of pristine and microbially colonized (biofouled) 6 and 45 µm polystyrene microbeads and 100 µm polyethylene terephthalate microfibers on survival, reproduction, accumulation and microbial community composition and diversity in two freshwater benthic macroinvertebrates, the oligochaete worm *Tubifex tubifex* and the pulmonate snail *Planorbella pilsbryi.* I also examined the temporal patterns of MPs ingestion and excretion in *Planorbella pilsbryi* to better understand how MPs characteristics influence these processes. Together, this research aims to address important knowledge gaps surrounding the effects of MPs exposure and the potential for accumulation in freshwater benthic macroinvertebrates and will contribute to ecological risk assessments of MPs in freshwater ecosystems. 




